{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BqHYLnfk4ib2eLvut",
	"notes": "A Painboy (Dok): the Ork battlefield medic, whose methods are brutal, unhygienic by any human standard, and effective on Ork patients. Built on the standard Boy chassis; a \"Big Dok\" would add the Growth Lens: Nob the same way a Big Mek does. Point math: Ork Physiology 179 + Painboy's Eye (level 2) 20 + skills 15 = 214 points, inside framework.md's Elite human tier (200-350).\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "T2E3bgRuzcDfMNIwl",
			"name": "Ork Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork Boy: the baseline greenskin warrior, grown fast, fed on violence, and built to get bigger and meaner the longer it survives. This trait is the shared baseline every growth lens (Nob, Warboss) and every Oddboy specialisation (Mek, Painboy, Weirdboy) builds on top of. Primary sources: Codex: Orks background across editions, Nate Crowley's Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! and Mike Brooks' Brutal Kunnin', calibrated against Dark Heresy/Rogue Trader/Deathwatch/Only War Ork material for relative scale.\n\nDesign: 138 (Attribute Modifiers) + 10 (Thick Hide) + 10 (High Pain Threshold) + 6 (Hard to Kill 3) + 5 (Rapid Healing) + 5 (Injury Tolerance, No Brain) + 15 (Resistant to Disease and Toxins) + 0 (Fungal Physiology) - 10 (Bloodlust) - 10 (Impulsiveness) - 5 (Overconfidence) + 10 (Ork Kunnin' 1) + 5 (WAAAGH! Gestalt) = 179 points of physiology alone, before skills — see the Ork Boy template for a full character, and the growth lenses and Oddboy Talents below for Nobs, Warbosses, Meks, Painboyz and Weirdboyz built on top of it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t3sjd1ooPxTiWCV2a",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Boy)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: ST 22, DX 11, IQ 8, HT 13, HP 26, SM 0. Increased ST +12 (B14, 10/level, 120) for ST 22 — \"stooped but heavy; brawnier than a man\": Basic Lift 96 lb, well past an unaugmented human but nowhere near an Astartes' ST 30. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 11 — \"far clumsier than a Marine,\" barely above a Guardsman's own DX 12 despite the far greater ST. Decreased IQ -2 (B16, 20/level, -40) for IQ 8, the framework's own figure for \"not smart, but not helpless either\" — offset in play by Ork Kunnin' below rather than by raw IQ. Increased HT +3 (B15, 10/level, 30) for HT 13. Extra Hit Points +4 (B16, 2/level, 8) for HP 26 (22 base from ST, +4): an Ork soaks up more damage than its ST alone would suggest, matching how much punishment a Boy shrugs off in the fiction. 120+20-40 +30+8 = 138 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 138,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 12
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "telgBDj7Z1hBFwLqO",
					"name": "Thick Hide",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 2, all locations. Stacks with worn armour.\n\nLore: An Ork's skin is thick, rubbery and mottled green, tough enough on its own to turn a knife-edge or a glancing shot that would open a human. Every depiction of an unarmoured Ork brawling — Codex background, Crowley's and Brooks' fiction alike — treats a bit of greenskin hide as meaningfully tougher than bare human flesh, well before any scrap armour goes on top of it.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 2 (B46, 5/level, all locations, 10 points): modest next to the Ossmodula's partial DR 3 or Custodes-tier toughness, but applies everywhere and is on top of whatever armour an Ork straps on.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t3w-fsX8-ECUbrkyT",
					"name": "High Pain Threshold",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks shrug off wounds, keep fighting with an arm hanging by a tendon, and treat a lost eye as a minor inconvenience worth a good story later — a running joke across every Ork-focussed novel and a staple of Codex background (\"'e's still comin'!\").\n\nDesign: Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59), 10 points, unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tvl-nOavJCnaG95XR",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "+3 to HT rolls made solely to avoid death.\n\nLore: An Ork that should be dead — gutshot, missing limbs, skull dented in — routinely keeps swinging until the fight is over, and often survives afterwards besides. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! plays this for both horror and comedy in the same scene more than once.\n\nDesign: Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points): +3 to death checks, below a Warboss's own extra levels below.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "teBk8ozgM4kpY-l6o",
					"name": "Rapid Healing",
					"reference": "B79",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Wounds that would fester or kill close over in days rather than weeks — part of the same fungal biology that lets a beaten Ork mob be back at strength within a season. Codex background is explicit that Ork tissue heals unnaturally fast; Black Library plays it as \"give it a week and 'e'll be grand.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rapid Healing (B79, 5 points): doubles effective HT for natural recovery rolls.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "ta_QCdkIlQrx0Cpoh",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (No Brain)",
					"reference": "B61",
					"local_notes": "A hit to the skull or eye is resolved as a hit to the face instead; there is no separate brain to target for extra injury. House rule, not a further purchased trait: an Ork who loses a limb — or, per the fiction, even the head — to a crippling or removal wound is not necessarily dead from that alone. A Painboy (or any suitably brutal battlefield medic) who recovers the part and staples, bolts or wires it back on within a scene or two restores it to function once the patient heals, using Surgery per the framework's skill table. This is a GM-adjudicated narrative rule that Hard to Kill, Rapid Healing and this trait together make plausible, not a separate priced advantage.\n\nLore: Orks are famous for surviving injuries that would kill anything else outright, up to and including having a head sewn back on by a Doks after a losing argument — a beat repeated across Codex background, Brutal Kunnin' and countless tabletop-derived anecdotes (\"'e's had worse\"). Ork brain function is diffuse and fungal rather than centralised in the human sense, which is offered here as the setting explanation rather than a citable page reference.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance, No Brain (B61, 5 points): mechanically small on its own, but combined with Hard to Kill and Rapid Healing above it is what makes \"shrugs off a head wound\" true at the table instead of just in the fluff text.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "tY4s4Tashcbs8IcnL",
					"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Common: Disease and Toxins), Immunity.\n\nLore: An Ork can eat rotten meat, drink promethium-cut moonshine and wade through toxic industrial runoff without noticeable harm — Codex background treats Ork constitutions as effectively immune to the diseases and poisons that trouble other races, part of the same fungal-symbiote biology that lets a Squig be dinner, transport or weapon depending on how it's grown.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Common: Disease and Toxins, Immunity tier — base 15, x1 for Immunity = 15 points, the same tier and math this project's Oolitic Kidney entry uses for a single unified hazard category, pitched below the Custodes' broader Very Common tier (30) since Orks are shown getting stinking drunk and mildly green-sick on purpose for fun, not as flatly unaffected as a Custodian.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "tXgacJGw4FYuXlZOR",
					"name": "Fungal Physiology (Spore-Born)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks are not born but grown: an Ork reproduces by releasing spores that seed fresh fungal growth wherever conditions allow (a mechanism shared with Gretchin, Squigs and the rest of the Ork ecosystem), has no biological sex, and needs neither a mate nor, ordinarily, a trained physician who isn't another Ork with a saw and a good attitude. This is consistent, well-established Codex background and is played straight rather than for shock across Black Library's Ork fiction — an Ork simply is an ambulatory fungal colony shaped like a green thug.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag. Every mechanical payoff of this biology (thick hide, extreme pain tolerance, fast healing, survivability of wounds that would kill outright, immunity to disease and poison) is priced separately above; this entry exists purely to document why an Ork needs no gender, no mate, and no bedside manner from anyone.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t_USdXbI9t_yHCq2q",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Fighting is not a means to an end for an Ork, it is the point of being alive: Orks pick fights they don't need to, press an attack past the point of sense, and are consistently written (Ghazghkull Thraka above all) as unable to simply walk away from a fight worth having.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tYuL7gIBfFvWeAcRy",
					"name": "Impulsiveness",
					"reference": "B143",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks act first and, on a good day, think about it afterwards — a running theme in how Ork plans in the fiction go from \"good idea\" to \"already happening\" with nothing in between.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Impulsiveness (B143), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tLOfqmaFdBlDWqq1O",
					"name": "Overconfidence",
					"reference": "B94",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork mob genuinely believes it can win any fight it's in, right up until (and sometimes past) the point it's losing badly — a big part of why Ork strategy in the fiction is so often \"charge and find out.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B94), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "t9v4dS9p6usAxbNb1",
					"name": "Ork Kunnin'",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "This project's Talent (B89) for practical battlefield cunning: adds\nits level to Tactics, Traps, Camouflage and Observation, the same way\nthe Psyker Talent (Imperium/Traits/Psykers and the Warp.adq) adds to\nits own listed skills, per that file's house extension of Talent's\nnormal rules. An Ork Boy is not clever in the abstract (IQ 8 is IQ\n8), but a mob that has survived a hundred scraps has a bone-deep,\npractical instinct for where the enemy is weak, where the cover is,\nand when a trap will work — instinct this Talent represents directly\nrather than through raw IQ.\n\nLore: Ork tactics look like chaos from outside and are, by any human military-doctrine standard, chaos — and yet Ork mobs consistently find the gap in a defence, spring an ambush, or route around a strongpoint with an efficiency that surprises Imperial commanders who underestimate them. Codex background and both Crowley's and Brooks' novels play this as real, if inarticulate, battlefield cunning rather than dumb luck.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Psyker Talent uses for the same reason. Base entry costs 0 points at level 0; every template above Gretchin buys at least level 1 (10 points, included in the Ork Physiology package below).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tVAnG9C6DMco3rmnc",
					"name": "WAAAGH! Gestalt",
					"local_notes": "Roughly ten or more fellow Orks fighting nearby, in earshot of the\nsame battle-roar, count as a WAAAGH! for this bonus. A Weirdboy\ncaught up in a WAAAGH! also treats his own Warp Empowerment as one\nlevel higher (cap +10) for the purpose of which known powers he may\nsafely cast, per the Weirdboy template's notes — this is the\nmechanical hook for \"Orks believe it, so it works, and it works\nbetter the more of them believe it together.\"\n\nLore: A lone Ork is dangerous; a mob of Orks worked up into a full WAAAGH! is a weaponised belief field — braver, louder, and (per Codex background's long-running joke about Ork technology) more likely to have its crude guns and rickety vehicles actually function, because enough Orks are collectively certain that they will. Weirdboyz in particular are consistently shown drawing directly on this collective psychic roar to power their biggest, most dangerous tricks; a Weirdboy isolated from a WAAAGH! is a much smaller threat.\n\nDesign: A narrow conditional Will/Fright Check bonus, eyeballed against Basic's Fearlessness (2/level flat, B55) but restricted to a specific, common-in-play situation rather than always on, so priced well below a flat level of Fearlessness at 5 points. The Weirdboy-specific Empowerment bump is documented, not separately priced, since it only changes which already-bought powers a Weirdboy may safely reach for.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "conditional_modifier",
							"situation": "to resist Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp, while fighting as part of a large mob of fellow Orks (a WAAAGH!)",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tUKDayeEcJ1We_vHT",
			"name": "Painboy's Eye",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Adds its level to Surgery (Ork Methods), Physician (Ork Methods) and\nDiagnosis, the same house extension of Talent's rules the Psyker\nTalent uses. Ork medicine is brutal, fast and effective on Ork\npatients specifically — bone saws, magnets, and a total disregard for\nanaesthetic — and this Talent is what makes a Painboy's crude methods\nactually work on greenskin physiology as well as (or better than) a\ncareful human surgeon's would.\n\nLore: Painboyz (Doks) are Ork medics, and Ork medicine is not for the squeamish: replacement limbs bolted on with mismatched parts, \"brain surgery\" with a chisel, and patients who by any human medical standard should not have survived the treatment, let alone the original injury. Codex background and Black Library both play this completely straight — it works, on Orks, because a Painboy's tools and instincts are matched to Ork biology in a way finesse never would be.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting three skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket, same bracket as Ork Kunnin' and Mek Instinct with one skill to spare). Base entry 0 points at level 0; the Painboy template buys level 2 (20 points).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2,
			"base_points": 0
		}
	],
	"skills": [
		{
			"id": "sf9ynE9F4pDfBTR0Q",
			"name": "Surgery",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Bone saws, magnets, and total disregard for anaesthetic — and it works, on Orks.\n\nDesign: IQ+0 (8) for 4 points, Hard; +2 more in practice from Painboy's Eye 2.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"specialization": "(Ork Methods)",
			"difficulty": "iq/h",
			"points": 4
		},
		{
			"id": "smfOC_Pm8YLDOf842",
			"name": "Physician",
			"local_notes": "Lore: General Ork field medicine, mostly a matter of knowing how much abuse an Ork body can absorb.\n\nDesign: IQ+0 (8) for 4 points, Hard; +2 more in practice from Painboy's Eye 2.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
